// ============================================================================
== 4.1 The Six Claims

A theme is not a topic. *Migration* is a topic; *migration can repeat an old departure on worse terms* is a theme. The paper's theme options are written as claims, and the wrong ones are usually claims the book never makes.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch04_themes.svg",
  "The six themes, what the novel actually says about each, and where to point when a question asks for evidence.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The theme test")[
  + *A claim, not a subject.* If the option is one word — *education*, *corruption*, *family* — it is a topic and it is the wrong shape of answer.
  + *Supported by more than one scene.* A theme is what several chapters add up to; one scene is an episode.
  + *The whole book, not a character.* What Seri thinks is not what the novel thinks.
  + *Costed.* This novel's themes always carry a price: staying costs a marriage, leaving costs a mission, integrity costs a job.
]

// ============================================================================
== 4.2 Japa and the New Slavery

The novel gives the case for leaving every good line in the book. Seri earns about £10,000
a month; the post waiting for Bepo pays about £3,600; his salary is ₦400,000 and the
marriage is already strained. His colleagues say what everyone says: it is a golden
opportunity.

Then it gives the other side, and the other side is not an argument but a place.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch04_japa.svg",
  "The japa argument, both ways, and what actually settles it. Note that the decision is not made on the arithmetic.", width: 100%)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch04_heritage.svg",
  "Badagry carries three things at once: a fact, an analogy and an irony the narrator never resolves.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("The analogy, in one paragraph")[
  At the Black Heritage Museum in Badagry, standing at the Point of No Return where enslaved Africans were put on ships, Bepo is struck by a rhyme he cannot unhear: his ancestors were taken, and he is going willingly. The novel does not say the two are the same. It says that a man who has seen the first cannot walk onto the second without hearing it — and Chapter 12 makes that literal by putting the dream of the slave ship in his seat on the plane.
]

#trap-box("The book is not a pamphlet against travel")[
  The novel is careful to show migration succeeding as well as failing. Sola has done well in the United Kingdom and tells Bepo the transition will be smooth. What the novel distrusts is not departure but *departure without a reckoning* — leaving because everyone is leaving, without asking what is being left.
]

// ============================================================================
== 4.3 Integrity in Education

The novel's argument about education is made by comparison. Put Beesway beside Stardom and
the difference is not money or results; it is whether anyone can correct the person in
charge.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch04_integrity.svg",
  "Beesway and Stardom under four tests. Stardom is not perfect; it is answerable, and that is the whole difference.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("Three refusals, and what each one costs")[
  + At *Beesway*, Bepo refuses to accept the director's ritual. He is struck on the head and leaves the school.
  + At *Fruitful Future*, he refuses Mr Ogo's offer of a rite for ₦35,000. The parent withdraws his child, the roads fail, and the school closes.
  + At *Stardom*, he refuses to let a teacher be sacked for a sentence the teacher got right. It costs him nothing — and that is the novel's quiet hope: in a school that can be argued with, integrity is survivable.
]

#rule-box("What the novel says about corruption in education")[
  + It is not usually a bribe in an envelope. It is a parent's certainty, an owner's vanity, a board's fear and a queue at the passport office.
  + It is *structural* before it is personal: the schools in the book fail because of roads, fees, superstition and class, not because one man is wicked.
  + It is survivable where there are rules and answerable where there is a board — which is why Stardom, with all its faults, is the novel's model.
]

// ============================================================================
== 4.4 Leadership, Family and Belonging

Three themes remain, and they are the ones the character questions borrow most often.

*Leadership and its price.* Bepo is a good leader and the novel shows the bill: he weeps in
public, he hides a decision for five days, he is lonely in the job. Authority in this book
is not a reward; it is a weight that the holder carries in front of an assembly.

*Family against duty.* Seri and the children are not a distraction from the plot; they are
the other half of it. The novel refuses to make either side wrong. A man may love his
family and his work, and the two may still pull him in opposite directions — that is the
adult content of the book, and the reason it is set for candidates rather than children.

*Belonging.* The excursions in Chapter 9 exist so that the students can see the country
they belong to before they think of leaving it. Bepo's line to the children who laugh at
Mushin — that being born in such a place condemns nobody — is the novel's statement of
what education is for.

#shortcut-box("Three sentences that answer most theme questions")[
  + *“The novel is about the cost of leaving and the cost of staying.”*
  + *“It argues that a school stays honest only while somebody is allowed to correct the person in charge.”*
  + *“It uses Badagry to say that modern migration repeats an older departure.”*

  Learn those three sentences and attach a scene to each. That is a theme answer.
]

// ============================================================================
== 4.5 Setting, and What a Place Is For

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch04_settings.svg",
  "Six settings, what each one is, and what it carries. Only one of them is abroad, and the reader never goes there.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("Why the novel never takes us to London")[
  Seri's London is a voice on a telephone and a set of figures. The reader is never shown it, because the book is not about whether London is good; it is about what Nigeria is worth to the man leaving it.

  A question that asks where the novel is set wants *Lekki, Lagos*; a question that asks where Seri lives wants *the United Kingdom*; a question that asks which setting the reader never visits wants *London*.
]

#rule-box("The setting questions, and where each answer lives")[
  + *Main setting:* Stardom Schools, Lekki, Lagos State.
  + *The past:* Beesway Group of Schools, and Fruitful Future, the school he co-founded.
  + *Heritage:* Badagry — the Akran's palace, the First Storey Building, the Black Heritage Museum, the Point of No Return.
  + *The state:* the immigration office in Ibadan, the NIN desk, the Lagos–Ibadan Expressway.
  + *Abroad:* the United Kingdom — named, discussed, and never shown.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The main setting",
  [The action of the novel takes place mainly in

    A. Lekki, Lagos \
    B. Ibadan \
    C. London \
    D. Abuja],
  [
    *Step 1 — the shape.* A setting fact, and one of the two easiest questions the section can ask.

    *Step 2 — the text.* Stardom Schools is in Lekki, in Lagos State, and the title of the book says so.

    *Step 3 — the traps.* Ibadan is where the passport office is; London is where Seri and the children live; Abuja is not a setting in the novel at all. All three are plausible to a candidate who has read a summary rather than the book.

    *Check.* Ask where the *scenes* happen, not where the characters are from. The answer is *A*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The significance of Badagry",
  [The visit to Badagry in Chapter 9 is significant mainly because it

    A. gives the students a holiday from school \
    B. draws a parallel between the slave trade and modern migration \
    C. shows that Bepo is a good organiser \
    D. introduces the character of the Akran of Badagry],
  [
    *Step 1 — the evidence.* The chapter spends its emotional weight on the museum and the Point of No Return, and Bepo leaves it unable to stop thinking about the comparison.

    *Step 2 — the claim.* The analogy is the point: a people once taken are now leaving willingly, and a man who has seen the first cannot ignore the second.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A is the cynical reading of a school trip; C is true and irrelevant; D is a real figure who appears for a page. None of them explains why the chapter ends where it does.

    *Check.* Ask what the episode is doing in the book, not what happens in it. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "What the cooperative episode illustrates",
  [The discovery of the teachers' cooperative society chiefly illustrates

    A. the dishonesty of Nigerian teachers \
    B. the board's fear of a financially independent staff \
    C. the failure of the school's academic standards \
    D. Bepo's inability to manage money],
  [
    *Step 1 — the scene.* The staff have saved ₦95 million and bought cars. The board's reaction is not relief but alarm, and the chairman's proverb — a snake hung in the roof — names the fear: a staff with money might leave and start a rival school.

    *Step 2 — the claim.* The episode is about class and control, not about theft. Nothing in the chapter says the money was stolen.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A is a slur the text does not support; C belongs to a different chapter; D is contradicted by the fact that the cooperative is the staff's, not the principal's.

    *Check.* If an option turns a scene about *fear* into a scene about *crime*, it has changed the subject. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The novel's attitude",
  [The novel's attitude to Nigerian education is best described as

    A. entirely despairing \
    B. uncritically admiring \
    C. critical but hopeful \
    D. indifferent],
  [
    *Step 1 — the evidence for criticism.* Beesway's ritual, Fruitful Future's collapse, the queue at the passport office, the parent who nearly ends a career over a correct sentence.

    *Step 2 — the evidence for hope.* Stardom has a board that argues and an MD who can be corrected; the excursions work; the students carry their principal on their shoulders; the last chapter is called *Dawn*.

    *Step 3 — the absolutes.* *Entirely* and *uncritically* are the two options the book spends its length disproving, and *indifferent* is impossible for a novel this angry.

    *Check.* When two extremes are offered, the answer is usually the third option. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "The theme that is not there",
  [Which of the following is LEAST supported by the novel?

    A. Migration can be a reasonable choice and still be a loss. \
    B. A school's integrity depends on whether its owner can be corrected. \
    C. Foreign education is superior to Nigerian education. \
    D. A teacher's work outlives the teacher.],
  [
    *Step 1 — mark the negative.* LEAST supported: three options are claims the novel makes, and one is a claim it never makes.

    *Step 2 — the three.* A is the whole of Chapters 2 to 4; B is the Beesway contrast; D is the carver's proverb in Chapter 11.

    *Step 3 — the odd one.* C sounds like something a character might say — Seri might, and so might the parent in Chapter 4 who wants his children educated abroad — but the novel never endorses it. A plausible opinion voiced by a character is not a theme.

    *Check.* Attribute the claim. If it belongs to a character and not to the book, it is not a theme. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/recap_ch04.svg",
  "Chapter 4 recap card: theme and setting.", width: 74%)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 4 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    Six claims hold the novel: japa repeats an old departure; integrity in education is expensive; leadership is a weight; family and duty pull against each other; the wish for success without work is the corruption that frightens the book most; and a country must be seen to be loved. Each claim has a scene — Badagry, Beesway, the assembly, the telephone to Seri, the cow in the ground, the excursion to Mushin. Six settings carry them: Lekki and Stardom, Beesway and Fruitful Future, Badagry, Ibadan, and a London the reader never visits. A theme is a claim with more than one scene behind it; a setting is a place with a meaning, not a postcode.
  ]
]

== 4.6 Chapter 4 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [The novel's treatment of migration suggests that], [migration is always a mistake], [migration is always an improvement], [migration can be reasonable and still be a loss], [migration is irrelevant to the story])

#mcq(2, [Badagry is chiefly associated in the novel with], [the transatlantic slave trade], [the first university in Nigeria], [a waterfall in Osun State], [the school's excursion budget])

#mcq(3, [Which of the following places is mentioned but never shown in the novel?], [Lekki], [Badagry], [Ibadan], [London])

#mcq(4, [The contrast between Beesway and Stardom suggests that a school's integrity depends on], [the size of its fees], [whether its owner can be corrected], [the number of its pupils], [the quality of its buildings])

#answer-key-grid(("C", "A", "D", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("C", [The novel makes the case for leaving and then shows what it costs. A and B are the two extremes it spends the book refusing; D contradicts four chapters.]),
  ("A", [The Black Heritage Museum, the Point of No Return, the Akran's palace. B is an invention, C confuses Badagry with the waterfalls of Chapter 9, and D is a joke the text does not make.]),
  ("D", [London is spoken of, telephoned and dreamed about, and never visited. A, B and C are all scenes in the book — which is why they look safe.]),
  ("B", [Correction is the difference the two schools are built to show. A, C and D are measurable things the novel never treats as the cause.]),
))

